WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:06.100
Disney gets down to business with the live-action
remake of their animated Mulan, but is it

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:08.719
as powerful, pleasurable, and indestructible
as the original?

00:00:08.719 --> 00:00:13.640
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and today
I'm explaining the 14 biggest differences

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.980
between the new movie and the 1998 classic.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:19.699
Some spoilers ahead, so take care if you haven't
seen it yet.

00:00:19.699 --> 00:00:24.279
He may be a fan favourite but that didn't
save the much-loved Mushu from being cut from

00:00:24.279 --> 00:00:25.279
the new film.

00:00:25.279 --> 00:00:26.279
"Dishonor!

00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:27.619
Dishonor on your whole family."

00:00:27.619 --> 00:00:32.250
Brilliantly voiced by Eddie Murphy, the travel-sized
dragon with a larger-than-life personality

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:36.989
brought a wealth of memorable moments and
hilarious comic relief to the animated movie.

00:00:36.989 --> 00:00:42.329
"I am the powerful, the pleasurable, the indestructible
Mushu."

00:00:42.329 --> 00:00:47.120
However, his comedic tone didn't fit the style
of the live-action film which leans more towards

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.489
epic martial arts war movie.

00:00:49.489 --> 00:00:54.590
And, crucially, while many western Disney
fans love Mushu, according to producer Jason

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:58.390
Reed, his silly shenanigans didn't play well
to audiences in China

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:03.079
where dragons are important symbols of power,
strength and good fortune, in contrast to

00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:07.070
Western mythology where they've often been
seen as dangerous and aggressive creatures

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:08.360
to be defeated.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:12.000
The new Mulan does still have a "mythological
sidekick of sorts" though.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.910
This time her guardian is a phoenix, a silent
character that guides her on her journey to

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:19.570
duty, honour, and being true to herself.

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:24.290
In Chinese tradition, the phoenix is often
regarded as a symbol of female power and together

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:28.650
with the dragon the pair of mythological creatures
sit on either side of the emperor.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:32.640
There are some beautiful shots of the phoenix
appearing at crucial moments in Mulan's story.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.810
And although it is sad to lose the iconic
character of Mushu, his replacement with the

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:41.890
phoenix does better suit this more serious
version of the tale.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:46.720
The remake introduces several brand new characters
and my favourite of these is the sorceress,

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:50.790
played by Gong Li, who's an ally of the movie's
main villain Böri Khan.

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:54.960
She's a powerful and intriguing figure and
the way she shapeshifts into birds creates

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.680
some truly arresting visuals and is also a
hat-tip to villain Shan Yu's falcon in the

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.020
original movie.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.700
Of course, she's much more crucial to the
plot than the 1998 film's bird-of-prey,

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.530
with her shapeshifting and magical ability
to take over another human body proving integral

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.420
to the villains' strategy.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:17.070
And her character also adds an interesting
parallel with Mulan, balancing the protagonist's

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:21.670
story as she knows what it feels like to be
ostracised for being different and powerful,

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:26.490
and her arc enhances the themes of identity
and belonging so crucial to the film.

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:30.530
When it came to Mulan's lucky sidekick Cri-Kee
who communicates via chirps

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:32.530
(CHIRPS)
"What you mean, a loser?"

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:36.870
rather than cutting him out of the new film
entirely, Disney took a different approach,

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:41.290
keeping his spirit alive by adding a brand
new human character, who's appropriately called

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:42.290
Cricket.

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:46.060
Cricket is a fellow army recruit whose mother
says he's a good-luck charm.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:51.080
And just like the animated bug unwittingly
got Mulan into trouble, the new Cricket also

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:55.920
accidentally causes a minor ruckus at the
garrison as the new recruits line up.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.620
And like his animated insect counterpart,
this Cricket also helps Mulan out at some

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:01.980
key moments.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:06.670
He's a sweet new character who adds some nice
comedic touches to the film, which makes the

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:10.680
movie's fake-out at the end all the more unbearable!

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:15.170
Like Mushu, one character that fans may find
themselves missing is Army Captain and love

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:16.209
interest Li Shang.

00:03:16.209 --> 00:03:20.900
For the live-action version, his character
has been split in two, with Donnie Yen playing

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:26.410
Commander Tung, Mulan's superior and mentor;
and actor Yoson An taking on the role of Honghui,

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:30.750
a trainee soldier, who, after a rocky start,
"sees that there's something different about

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:34.040
Mulan" compared to the others, and ends up
a close ally.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:38.650
According to the film's producer Reed, the
filmmakers felt it was inappropriate for Mulan's

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:41.740
commanding officer to be her love interest
in the remake,

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:46.430
so they removed the power differential between
them making Honghui her equal in the army.

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:50.069
And similar to the animation, there are some
hints at something more between them.

00:03:50.069 --> 00:03:51.560
"What if she doesn't like me?"

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:52.560
"She will."

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.420
"I mean I think she will, you know.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:56.870
You never know with women."

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:59.830
Interestingly, the film's hair and make-up
designer has said they

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:03.910
"went for a young, clean-shaven look that
was both feminine and masculine" for Honghui

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:06.440
so that
"Mulan would fit in better when she joins

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:08.720
up and pretends to be male" and also because
his

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:11.020
"character has a fragility to him" that they
wanted to keep.

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:15.861
When Reed was asked whether his character
would be regarded as a potential LGBTQ icon

00:04:15.861 --> 00:04:20.019
in the way Li Shang is, he said
"it'll play the same way as it does in the

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:22.680
animated film" as they used that as their
model.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:26.870
Given the way the live-action movie ends though,
it feels like the romantic connection between

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:29.110
the two is downplayed compared to the original.

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:30.949
"Would you like to stay for dinner?"

00:04:30.949 --> 00:04:32.789
"Would you like to stay forever?"

00:04:32.789 --> 00:04:34.029
"Dinner would be great."

00:04:34.029 --> 00:04:39.270
The new film sticks to the main story beats
of the 1998 one, but unlike some other Disney

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:43.939
live-action adaptations, it isn't a shot for
shot remake, so expect some well-known scenes

00:04:43.939 --> 00:04:44.939
to get the chop.

00:04:44.939 --> 00:04:50.199
For example, the animation's iconic scene
of Mulan slicing off her long locks to disguise

00:04:50.199 --> 00:04:53.409
herself as a soldier doesn't make it into
the live-action movie.

00:04:53.409 --> 00:04:58.789
For many Western audiences, cutting her hair
short was an empowering moment, symbolising

00:04:58.789 --> 00:05:02.940
her leaving behind her old life and its rigid
gender-based expectations.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:08.339
However, that moment rang false to audiences
in China as in their culture "male warriors

00:05:08.339 --> 00:05:09.809
wore their hair long".

00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:14.289
So, the new film takes the animated scene
and gives it a twist, creating its own new

00:05:14.289 --> 00:05:18.389
empowering moment for our heroine
when she decides to let her hair loose and

00:05:18.389 --> 00:05:20.840
willingly reveal her true identity.

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:25.349
This moment comes just after Mulan's one-to-one
encounter with the sorceress who accuses her

00:05:25.349 --> 00:05:29.050
of hiding who she really is:
"Your deceit weakens you.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:31.250
It poisons your chi."

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:36.120
Seeing her tresses flow free as she fights
fiercely on the battlefield is a moving moment

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:40.659
because Mulan has made her own choice to unmask
herself to the world.

00:05:40.659 --> 00:05:45.199
By contrast, in the animation, she's forced
out of her male disguise and unintentionally

00:05:45.199 --> 00:05:48.779
reveals herself as a woman after being injured
in battle.

00:05:48.779 --> 00:05:53.729
In the new version, Mulan gets to take back
her power, embrace her full identity, and

00:05:53.729 --> 00:05:58.509
make her own decision as to when it's time
to reveal who she really is.

00:05:58.509 --> 00:06:02.680
Another memorable moment in the animated movie
comes after Li Shang tells our heroine she's

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:05.779
not good enough to be a soldier and orders
her to go home.

00:06:05.779 --> 00:06:10.669
Dejected, she begins to leave but then decides
to retrieve the arrow from the pole that everyone

00:06:10.669 --> 00:06:12.789
has so far failed to climb.

00:06:12.789 --> 00:06:17.449
Using her smarts, she repurposes the heavy
weights to help her scale the pole and reach

00:06:17.449 --> 00:06:18.570
the arrow.

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:22.789
In the live-action, the pole is gone, but
the incredibly difficult task this time is

00:06:22.789 --> 00:06:27.460
to walk all the way up an enormous mountain
carrying heavy pails of water.

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:31.879
The interesting difference between the two
movies is that, in the animation, it's more

00:06:31.879 --> 00:06:36.949
Mulan's ingenuity and determination that help
her succeed in the seemingly impossible task.

00:06:36.949 --> 00:06:41.099
Whereas in the live-action, it's her chi that
powers her up the mountain summit.

00:06:41.099 --> 00:06:46.119
Overall, the new movie emphasises the power
of Mulan's chi as making her special, which

00:06:46.119 --> 00:06:50.349
isn't to say that this Mulan isn't smart,
but it is a notable difference between the

00:06:50.349 --> 00:06:52.099
two films.

00:06:52.099 --> 00:06:56.689
The animation's big bad, the Hun Army and
their leader Shan Yu, have been replaced by

00:06:56.689 --> 00:06:59.629
Böri Khan and his Rouran fighters from the
North.

00:06:59.629 --> 00:07:04.199
Khan's described by director Niki Caro as
someone who it looks like everybody has tried

00:07:04.199 --> 00:07:06.060
to kill but nobody has succeeded.

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:11.330
And he's played by Jason Scott Lee who's starred
in Disney live-action adaptations before,

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:15.639
having brought Mowgli to life in the 1994
version of The Jungle Book.

00:07:15.639 --> 00:07:20.509
Another new character introduced in the latest
version is Mulan's younger sister.

00:07:20.509 --> 00:07:25.649
The filmmakers felt "it added a broader emotional
context and more motivation for [our heroine]",

00:07:25.649 --> 00:07:27.539
especially at the end of the film.

00:07:27.539 --> 00:07:32.039
On top of that, the differences between the
two sisters helped to highlight just how different

00:07:32.039 --> 00:07:33.779
Mulan is to other young women.

00:07:33.779 --> 00:07:38.629
But adding a new relative to the story meant
the wise-cracking grandmother from the animation

00:07:38.629 --> 00:07:42.919
"Who spit in her bean curd?"
got the boot as they wanted to keep the focus

00:07:42.919 --> 00:07:45.110
on Mulan's parents and sister instead.

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:50.070
In a change from the animated movie where
Mulan is already a young woman at the start,

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:52.530
the remake opens with her as a child.

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:56.699
This new way of introducing her gives us some
backstory for her character, demonstrating

00:07:56.699 --> 00:08:01.909
her athleticism and agility, and her strong
connection to her chi from an early age.

00:08:01.909 --> 00:08:04.499
Mulan's striking difference to the other children
in the village

00:08:04.499 --> 00:08:08.719
and the way the rest of her neighbours view
her gives us an insight into how society treats

00:08:08.719 --> 00:08:12.120
women who don't fit its norms and the problems
it creates for her parents.

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:14.419
"It is Mulan I worry about.

00:08:14.419 --> 00:08:16.340
They'll call her a witch."

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:20.960
As producer Reed says, "She has all these
great qualities [...] but she doesn't do things

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:22.960
the way a young girl is supposed to.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:27.649
And it isn't until she's dressed as a boy
that people encourage those things in her."

00:08:27.649 --> 00:08:30.999
This new opening to the story brings some
comedy to the film as well

00:08:30.999 --> 00:08:35.319
and also includes a cool call-back to the
scene in the original movie where Mulan's

00:08:35.319 --> 00:08:39.190
dog Little Brother helps with her chores looking
after the chickens.

00:08:39.190 --> 00:08:43.839
This time though it's a young Mulan herself
who energetically chases around with the chickens

00:08:43.839 --> 00:08:47.470
and races through the temple,
where a pile of fruit takes a tumble rather

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:51.250
than in the animation where it's her father's
tea cup that hits the floor.

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:55.300
That fun little sequence does mean there's
no canine character this time, but that's

00:08:55.300 --> 00:09:00.130
understandable as this really isn't the type
of Disney film where animals performing household

00:09:00.130 --> 00:09:01.940
tasks fits in.

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:06.960
As for Mulan's horse, he's got very similar
colouring to her animated horse Khan;

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:11.250
but given the more realistic feel of this
film, don't expect the same expressive body

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:14.310
language or facial expressions!

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:17.070
"I'm working on it."

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:21.540
Oh, and his name in the new movie is actually
"Black Wind" because calling him Khan would

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:25.160
be very confusing given the main villain is
named Böri Khan.

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:30.639
In a bold move, the creative team behind Mulan
chose to move away from the musical genre,

00:09:30.639 --> 00:09:35.140
in complete contrast to many other recent
Disney remakes such as Beauty and the Beast,

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:36.649
Aladdin, and The Lion King.

00:09:36.649 --> 00:09:41.699
Although the new film does have fantastical
elements, it leans more heavily into epic

00:09:41.699 --> 00:09:46.610
war territory so the filmmakers wanted to
keep the world "more grounded" and "realistic".

00:09:46.610 --> 00:09:51.520
And they felt that Mulan bursting into song
to reflect on her situation or the imperial

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:55.190
soldiers busting out a tune
while getting down to business during a training

00:09:55.190 --> 00:09:57.870
montage wouldn't set the right tone.

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:02.390
While we don't get any show-stopping musical
numbers, we do get some instrumental tributes

00:10:02.390 --> 00:10:06.110
to the classic songs in the movie's score
and also in its dialogue.

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:10.009
And if you want to know where they all appear,
the details are in my Mulan easter eggs and

00:10:10.009 --> 00:10:11.470
things you missed video.

00:10:11.470 --> 00:10:14.589
Tap here to watch that or follow the link
in the video description.

00:10:14.589 --> 00:10:16.959
I'll add here it as soon as it's ready.

00:10:16.959 --> 00:10:21.399
On top of that, during the end credits, we
hear two new versions of Reflection,

00:10:21.399 --> 00:10:26.250
one by Christina Aguilera who also performed
the song for the animation and one in Mandarin

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:28.040
by Mulan star Yifei Liu.

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:33.460
Plus, Aguilera also performs a brand new song,
Loyal Brave True, written specially for the

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:37.310
remake and inspired by the virtues engraved
on Mulan's sword.

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:42.360
So, what do you think about the new Mulan
movie and how do you feel about the changes?

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:44.350
Leave your thoughts in the comments below.

00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:46.579
If you enjoyed this then do leave a thumbs-up.

00:10:46.579 --> 00:10:49.120
It really helps and I hugely appreciate it.

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:53.470
Tap left for my next Mulan video or tap right
for something else you're sure to like.

00:10:53.470 --> 00:10:55.690
Thanks for watching and see ya next time.

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:57.079
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

